        content: `
            <p class="mb-6 italic text-slate-500 font-medium">Love doesn’t fade overnight. It fades quietly—through missed conversations, unspoken appreciation, and small moments that slowly stop happening.</p>
            
            <p class="mb-6">Most husbands don’t mean to make their wives feel unloved. Life gets busy. Work piles up. Stress takes over. And without realizing it, the relationship shifts from connection to routine.</p>
            
            <p class="mb-6">If you’ve ever wondered how to make your wife feel truly loved again, the answer isn’t complicated—but it does require intention.</p>
            
            <p class="mb-12">Let’s talk about what actually makes a difference.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">It Starts With Attention, Not Effort</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">You don’t need grand gestures to make your wife feel loved. What she notices more is where your attention goes.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">Are you listening when she talks, or just nodding while scrolling on your phone?</p>
            <p class="mb-8 font-bold text-slate-900 border-l-4 border-blue-400 pl-6">Real listening—eye contact, small responses, remembering details—creates a sense of emotional closeness that no expensive gift can replace. When she feels heard, she feels valued. And when she feels valued, love naturally follows.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Love Is in the Small, Everyday Moments</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">There’s a common mistake many people make: saving their effort for “special occasions.”</p>
            <p class="mb-6 font-black uppercase text-xs tracking-widest text-slate-400">Anniversaries. Birthdays. Big surprises.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">Those are great—but they’re not what sustain a relationship.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">Love grows in the ordinary moments:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-3 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>A simple “How was your day?” that you genuinely care about</li>
                <li>A quick compliment before she even asks</li>
                <li>Noticing the little things she does</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-12">These small actions might seem insignificant to you, but to her, they build emotional security over time.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Consistency Beats Intensity Every Time</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Being amazing once in a while doesn’t create a strong relationship. Being reliable, kind, and present every day does.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">You don’t have to be perfect. You just have to be consistent. A husband who shows up daily—with patience, respect, and care—will always make his wife feel more loved than someone who relies on occasional grand gestures.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Emotional Support Is Everything</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Sometimes, your wife doesn’t need advice. She doesn’t need solutions. She just needs you to sit beside her and say, “I understand.”</p>
            <p class="mb-6">When she’s overwhelmed, stressed, or upset, your response matters more than you think. Dismissing her feelings—even unintentionally—can create distance. But validating them? That builds trust.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">Being emotionally available is one of the deepest forms of love you can offer.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Partnership Matters More Than Words</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Saying “I love you” is important. But what matters more is whether your actions reflect it.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">Helping without being asked. Taking responsibility. Sharing the mental and physical load of daily life.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">When your wife feels like she’s not alone in managing everything, she feels supported. And support is a powerful form of love.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Don’t Underestimate Physical Affection</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Love isn’t always spoken. Sometimes it’s felt through the smallest physical gestures.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">Holding her hand while walking. A hug at the end of a long day. Sitting a little closer than usual. These moments create a quiet sense of connection that words often can’t express.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">See Her as More Than Just Your Wife</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Your wife has her own dreams, interests, and identity beyond the relationship.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">When you support her growth, respect her opinions, and encourage what makes her happy, you show her that you truly see her—not just as a partner, but as a person. That kind of respect deepens love in a lasting way.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">A Little Surprise Goes a Long Way</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">You don’t need to plan something extravagant to make her smile. Sometimes, it’s as simple as:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-3 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>Bringing her favorite snack</li>
                <li>Sending a thoughtful message during the day</li>
                <li>Planning a quiet evening together</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-12">Unexpected effort reminds her that she’s on your mind—even in the middle of a busy day.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Love Should Be Said—and Shown</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Never assume she already knows how you feel. Say “I love you.” Say it often. Say it sincerely.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">But more importantly, let your actions match your words. Because over time, what you do will always speak louder than what you say.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Making your wife feel loved isn’t about being perfect. It’s about being present.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">It’s in the way you listen, the way you respond, the way you show up—every single day. You don’t need to change everything at once. Just start small. Be a little more attentive. A little more expressive. A little more intentional.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">Because in the end, love isn’t built in grand moments. It’s built in the quiet, everyday ones that often go unnoticed—but mean everything.</p>
        `
