        content: `
            <p class="mb-6">Love is a universal feeling—but the way we express it isn’t the same for everyone.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">Have you ever felt like you were showing love, but the other person didn’t fully appreciate it? Or maybe you’ve wished someone would love you differently, but couldn’t explain how?</p>
            <p class="mb-6">That’s where love language types come in.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">Understanding them can completely change how you experience relationships—whether it’s with a partner, family, or even close friends.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Why Love Feels Different for Everyone</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Not everyone experiences love in the same way.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">For some people, hearing “I love you” means everything. For others, actions matter more than words. Some feel loved through time, while others through thoughtful gestures.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">This doesn’t mean one way is better than another—it just means people are wired differently when it comes to emotional connection.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">And when these differences aren’t understood, it can lead to confusion, frustration, and even distance in relationships.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">The 5 Main Love Language Types</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">The concept of love languages became popular because it simplifies something complex: how we give and receive love.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">Let’s explore each type in a way that actually makes sense in real life.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 text-slate-900">1. Words of Affirmation</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">For some people, words carry the most power. They feel loved when they hear:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-2 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>Compliments</li>
                <li>Encouragement</li>
                <li>Appreciation</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-6">A simple “I’m proud of you” or “You mean a lot to me” can have a deep emotional impact.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">On the other hand, harsh words or lack of verbal appreciation can hurt them more than you might expect.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 text-slate-900">2. Acts of Service</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">This love language is all about actions. People with this type feel loved when others:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-2 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>Help them with tasks</li>
                <li>Take responsibility without being asked</li>
                <li>Do things to make their life easier</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-6">For them, actions truly speak louder than words.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">Even small efforts—like helping with daily responsibilities—can mean a lot.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 text-slate-900">3. Receiving Gifts</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">This one is often misunderstood. It’s not about materialism—it’s about thoughtfulness.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">A meaningful gift shows:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-2 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>You were thinking about them</li>
                <li>You put effort into choosing something</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-6">It could be something small—a favorite snack, a handwritten note, or a simple surprise.</p>
            <p class="mb-12">For them, the value is in the meaning, not the price.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 text-slate-900">4. Quality Time</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Some people feel most loved when they have your full attention. Not just being around—but being present.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">This includes:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-2 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>Deep conversations</li>
                <li>Spending time without distractions</li>
                <li>Sharing experiences together</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-12">For them, time equals love.</p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-black mb-4 text-slate-900">5. Physical Touch</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">For others, love is best expressed through physical connection. Simple gestures like:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-2 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>Holding hands</li>
                <li>Hugging</li>
                <li>Sitting close</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-12">These create a sense of comfort, safety, and emotional closeness.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Why Understanding Love Languages Matters</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Here’s where things get interesting. Most people naturally express love in their own love language—not their partner’s.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">So if your love language is acts of service, you might show love by helping. But if your partner’s love language is words of affirmation, they might still feel unloved.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">That’s where misunderstandings begin.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">Understanding love languages helps you:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-2 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>Communicate better</li>
                <li>Avoid unnecessary conflict</li>
                <li>Make others feel truly valued</li>
            </ul>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">How to Discover Your Love Language</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">You don’t need a test to start figuring it out. Ask yourself:</p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mb-8 space-y-2 text-slate-700 font-medium">
                <li>What makes you feel most appreciated?</li>
                <li>What do you complain about most in relationships?</li>
                <li>How do you naturally show love to others?</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-12">Your answers often point directly to your love language.</p>

            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black mb-6 text-slate-900">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p class="mb-6">Love isn’t just about feeling—it’s about understanding.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">When you learn about different love language types, you stop guessing and start connecting in a way that actually works.</p>
            <p class="mb-6">You don’t need to change who you are. You just need to become more aware of how love is experienced—by you and by others.</p>
            <p class="mb-6 italic font-bold text-slate-700 font-medium border-l-4 border-pink-400 pl-6">Because sometimes, the problem isn’t a lack of love. It’s simply a difference in how that love is expressed.</p>
        `
